591 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504591 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504591 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f02f4648-14e0-5805-a7dd-9867416c6557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504600 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504600 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504600 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504600 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe2b2c06-359a-556f-b20c-5c316319fefe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504601 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504601 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342622" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504601 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504601 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $h(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{x+a}-\\sqrt{2+a}}, & x\\ne 2, \\\\ 6, & x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=2,$ what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504601 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0241cfe8-3868-5b51-b660-c610e52d9bb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504601 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the limit, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{x+a}-\\sqrt{2+a}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{x+a}-\\sqrt{2+a}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a}}{\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a})}{(\\sqrt{x+a})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2+a})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a})}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a})}{\\cancel{x-2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2+a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}h(x) & =h(2) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2+a} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt{2+a} & =3 \\\\ 2+a & =9 \\\\ a & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a=7$ removes the point discontinuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504603 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504603 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504603 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k), & x\\ne -1, \\\\ 3, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504603 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "060bce41-c046-5d08-898b-cfb8261e7661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504603 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(k-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(k-2) & =3 \\\\ k-2 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ k-2 & =125 \\\\ k & =127.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=127$ removes the point discontinuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504606 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504606 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504606 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504606 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{1-x}}{ax}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor what value of $a$ is the function continuous at $x=0?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504606 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d67e759-1525-5679-9c05-4036359d7add" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504606 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the limit, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{1-x}}{ax} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{1-x}}{ax}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x}}{\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-(\\sqrt{1-x})^{2}}{ax(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{ax(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{x}}{a\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{a(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{a(\\sqrt{0+1}+\\sqrt{1-0})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\ \\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a=4$ removes the point discontinuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
